const page = {
  title: "CHD-Z and CHD-W Genes in Bird Sex Determination",
  h1: "CHD-Z and CHD-W Genes in Bird Sex Determination",
  description: "What the CHD1 gametologs on the avian Z and W chromosomes are, how they diverged, and why they report chromosomal sex without determining it.",
  canonical: "/chd-z-chd-w-genes-bird-sexing/",
  author: "SENO Testing Center",
  date: "November 6, 2026",
};
const articleHtml = "<p>Most explanations of bird DNA sexing treat CHD-Z and CHD-W as if they were the bird's sex-determining genes. They are not. They are two copies of a housekeeping gene that happened to land on opposite sex chromosomes, and the laboratory uses their differences as a handle. The distinction sounds academic until a result disagrees with a bird's behaviour, breeding history or gonads. At that point, knowing what the marker can and cannot report is the whole argument.</p>\n<p>This page follows the biology from the gene to the gel. For the bench workflow, see <a href=\"/pcr-based-bird-dna-sexing/\">how PCR-based bird DNA sexing works</a>; for the wider picture, the <a href=\"/bird-dna-testing-methods-accuracy/\">methods and accuracy guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Two Genes, One Name</h2>\n<p>CHD1 stands for chromodomain helicase DNA-binding protein 1. It belongs to the SNF2 family of ATP-dependent chromatin remodellers, enzymes that slide and restructure nucleosomes so that transcription, replication and repair can reach the DNA. The protein carries a chromodomain, which binds methylated histone tails, and a helicase-like domain that uses ATP to move DNA.</p>\n<p>In mammals, CHD1 is a single-copy autosomal gene. In birds, the gene sits on both sex chromosomes: CHD1Z on the Z chromosome and CHD1W on the W. Two copies of one ancestral gene, separated by a sex chromosome pair that stopped recombining, are called gametologs. Protocols write them as CHD-Z and CHD-W, a shorthand that hides that they are variants of one locus, not two genes.</p>\n<p>That history explains why a single primer pair can amplify both. The exons stayed similar because both copies still encode a working remodeller; the introns drifted apart because nothing constrained them to match. The protein is a chromatin remodeller, not a sex-determination factor; its relevance to sexing is positional.</p>\n<h2>The Avian ZW System in Plain Terms</h2>\n<p>Birds reverse the mammalian pattern. Males are the homogametic sex (ZZ) and females are heterogametic (ZW). The W is a small, gene-poor chromosome that passes only from mother to daughter, while the Z passes from fathers to all offspring and from mothers to half of them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Z chromosome</th>\n<th>W chromosome</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Copies in a male</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copies in a female</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recombination</td>\n<td>Recombines along most of its length in males</td>\n<td>Largely non-recombining; inherited through females</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size and gene content</td>\n<td>Large, gene-rich, similar to autosomes</td>\n<td>A fraction of the Z, with few functional genes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inheritance</td>\n<td>Father to all chicks; mother to half</td>\n<td>Mother to daughters only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CHD1 copy</td>\n<td>CHD1Z</td>\n<td>CHD1W</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The pairing is ancient: Z and W diverged from a conventional chromosome pair early in avian evolution, and the W has been shedding genes since. CHD1W survived, which is why it became useful: conserved enough to amplify with Z-derived primers, divergent enough to give a different product length.</p>\n<h2>How CHD1W Survived on a Decaying Chromosome</h2>\n<p>The W chromosome does not recombine with the Z along most of its length, so it cannot shed deleterious mutations through crossover. Effective recombination on the W is close to zero, so selection is weak against genetic drift. Genes acquire stop codons and indels, and most are eventually lost.</p>\n<p>CHD1W avoided that fate in most living bird groups, but it did not escape it intact. Its introns accumulated repeats and indels, and its exonic sequence drifted away from CHD1Z. The drift is the useful part: it created the length difference that conventional sexing assays exploit. The retention is the lucky part: without a W copy, a Z-only primer pair would produce a single band in both sexes.</p>\n<h2>Where the W Copy Disappears</h2>\n<p>Where the retention fails, the assay fails with it. Species in which the W copy is missing, severely truncated or divergent at the primer site cannot be sexed with a primer pair designed for the canonical sequence. The bird still has a W chromosome; the marker simply is not there to report it.</p>\n<h2>Introns and the Size Difference Assays Depend On</h2>\n<p>Standard assays place primers in exons that flank an intron. Because the intron differs in length between CHD1Z and CHD1W, the amplicons differ in length, often by tens of base pairs and sometimes by only a few.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>CHD1Z</th>\n<th>CHD1W</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chromosome</td>\n<td>Z</td>\n<td>W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present in males</td>\n<td>Yes, two copies</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present in females</td>\n<td>Yes, one copy</td>\n<td>Yes, one copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exon sequence</td>\n<td>Reference</td>\n<td>Similar but diverged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intron length</td>\n<td>Species-specific</td>\n<td>Usually different from CHD1Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical assay signal</td>\n<td>One product in both sexes</td>\n<td>Second product in females only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Primer choice follows from this table. P2/P8 (Griffiths et al., 1998) amplifies a conserved region spanning an intron and was designed for a wide range of birds. The 2550F/2718R pair (Fridolfsson &amp; Ellegren, 1999) sits elsewhere and gives smaller products, which helps with degraded samples and with species where P2/P8 products sit too close together.</p>\n<p>Resolution is the other half. A 30 bp difference is comfortable on a 2% agarose gel; a 4 bp difference needs capillary electrophoresis, where an internal standard and fluorescent labels give base-level sizing. The <a href=\"/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/\">capillary electrophoresis guide</a> explains why that changes the confidence attached to a two-band call.</p>\n<h2>CHD Markers Report Sex; DMRT1 Determines It</h2>\n<p>The gene that actually drives avian gonadal fate is DMRT1, which sits on the Z chromosome. In the current model, two copies of DMRT1 in a ZZ embryo push the bipotential gonad toward testis, while a single copy in a ZW embryo leaves the default pathway open toward ovary. The mechanism is dosage-based, and it acts within the gonad rather than through a systemic hormone signal.</p>\n<p>Smith and colleagues (2009) tested the model directly. Reducing DMRT1 expression in ZZ chicken embryos feminised the gonads, which is strong evidence that the Z-linked dose is the decision point. Birds have no SRY, so the two systems found different molecular answers to the same problem.</p>\n<p>CHD1 sits outside that pathway entirely. It is a marker that happens to be on both sex chromosomes and is inherited with them. The confusion arises because laboratories report \"CHD-Z/CHD-W\" results in the same sentence as \"male/female\", which makes the marker sound like the cause. It is closer to a licence plate than an engine.</p>\n<h2>Turning Gametologs Into a Sex Call</h2>\n<p>An assay converts the biology into a decision rule. Primers are chosen so that the Z product falls in one size window and the W product in another. Reference males and females of the target species establish where those windows actually sit, since the same primer pair can produce different sizes in different taxa.</p>\n<p>At the bench, the rule is simple. Two products inside the expected windows mean ZW. One product at the Z size means ZZ, provided the same assay has produced a W product from reference females. Anything else, including a single product at an unexpected size or no product at all, is not a sex call until it is repeated or confirmed with a second marker.</p>\n<p>That last condition carries most of the weight. A primer pair that has never amplified a W product in a known female of the species has not demonstrated that it can detect a W chromosome. Its single-band results are male-compatible, not male-proving. The <a href=\"/the-complete-guide-to-bird-dna-sexing/\">complete guide to bird DNA sexing</a> covers sampling, and the <a href=\"/avian-molecular-biology-guide-the-foundation-of-bird-dna-testing/\">molecular biology guide</a> covers the amplification principles behind the calling rule.</p>\n<h2>When the Genotype and the Bird Disagree</h2>\n<p>Disagreements fall into three groups: the assay misread the sample, the sample did not come from the bird, or the bird's chromosomes and phenotype genuinely diverge.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Observation</th>\n<th>Likely explanation</th>\n<th>What it does not prove</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Female history, single Z band</td>\n<td>W dropout from primer mismatch or degradation</td>\n<td>That the bird is male</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Male history, extra band below the Z product</td>\n<td>Heteroduplex formation or non-specific product</td>\n<td>That the bird is female</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two bands, no breeding record</td>\n<td>ZW genotype with a reproductive problem</td>\n<td>That the laboratory erred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No product</td>\n<td>Failed extraction, inhibition or primer incompatibility</td>\n<td>That the bird is male</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Heteroduplexes mimic the female pattern. Late in the reaction, single strands from two CHD1Z variants can anneal into mismatched duplexes that migrate more slowly than either homoduplex. Casey and colleagues (2009) showed this artefact caused males to be scored as females under a standard protocol. A faint band above the Z product justifies a repeat with a different primer pair, not a female report.</p>\n<p>Gonadal sex reversal is a separate matter. A ZW bird with a masculinised gonad is still ZW, and a CHD assay will keep saying so. That is the correct result for the question the assay asks.</p>\n<h2>Where One Primer Pair Is Not Enough</h2>\n<p>Taxonomic coverage is the practical limit of CHD sexing, and the literature has been chipping away at the idea of a universal assay for years.</p>\n<p>Ratites sit outside the standard design. The widely used pairs were validated on non-ratite birds, and paleognaths such as ostriches and emus need different markers or extra confirmation. Applying a Neognathae pair to a ratite and reporting a single band is an untested inference.</p>\n<p>Parrots have their own complications. Kroczak and colleagues (2021) examined Psittaciformes and concluded that no single marker should be treated as universal for the order, recommending multiple markers to avoid misassignment. Their title calls the universal DNA marker a debunked myth, which is blunt but consistent with the data: some psittacine lineages carry CHD1W variants that weaken or shift the expected products.</p>\n<p>Pigeons illustrate the opposite case. Marc and colleagues (2025) validated pigeon-specific CHD1 primers and then tested them across other pet bird species, finding that performance travelled unevenly. A primer set optimised for one group is a starting point for another, not a finished assay. Breeders working with a single species are usually best served by a laboratory that has reference birds of that species, which is also the argument in <a href=\"/can-you-only-tell-a-birds-gender-with-a-dna-test/\">whether a DNA test alone can tell a bird's gender</a>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Are CHD-Z and CHD-W alleles or separate genes?</h3>\n<p>Gametologs: two copies of one ancestral gene on different sex chromosomes that no longer recombine. Allele implies alternative forms at one locus, which is not the case here.</p>\n<h3>Do all birds carry CHD-W?</h3>\n<p>Most non-ratite birds do, which is why the standard primers work widely. Some lineages have lost or modified the W copy, and ratites fall outside the validated range.</p>\n<h3>Why do males show one band and females two?</h3>\n<p>A male has two Z chromosomes and no W, so only CHD1Z is amplified. A female has one of each, so both products appear, provided the assay can separate them and the W copy amplifies efficiently.</p>\n<h3>Can a female ever show only one band?</h3>\n<p>Yes. W dropout from a primer-site mismatch, degraded DNA or preferential loss of the longer amplicon will produce a single Z band in a female. This is the most common direction of sexing error.</p>\n<h3>Is CHD1 the bird equivalent of SRY?</h3>\n<p>No. SRY triggers testis development in mammals. Birds use a Z-linked dosage mechanism centred on DMRT1, and CHD1 plays no part in gonadal fate. CHD1 is a marker that travels with the sex chromosomes.</p>\n<h3>Which primer set is best?</h3>\n<p>It depends on the species and the sample. P2/P8 covers a broad range, 2550F/2718R often gives better separation and smaller products, and species-specific designs outperform both where they exist. Ask the laboratory which pair it uses and what reference data it holds.</p>\n<h3>Can CHD sexing be wrong?</h3>\n<p>Yes, through W dropout, heteroduplex artefacts, contamination, sample mix-up or a primer that does not match the species. A result should be judged on the controls and the validation record, not on the band count alone.</p>\n<h3>Does CHD sexing work on embryos and eggshell membrane?</h3>\n<p>CHD markers are present from fertilisation, so embryo tissue and eggshell membrane can be tested. Success depends on sample quality and assay sensitivity; confirm the sample type first.</p>\n<h2>The Author's Take</h2>\n<p><strong>Position.</strong> CHD1 is a karyotype proxy, and the field would produce clearer reports if it said so on the first page.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> Almost every misleading claim about bird sexing starts with the assumption that the CHD test reads something fundamental about the bird. It reads two amplicon lengths. Those lengths track the Z and W chromosomes closely enough to be useful, but the moment a W copy fails to amplify, the assay reports a male with the same confidence it reports a real one. The multi-marker work in parrots and the pigeon primer validation point the same way: the marker is a tool with a species-specific service life, not a universal constant. Laboratories that name the primer, the species and the reference set are doing the honest version of this test.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure.</strong> This is editorial judgment grounded in the cited literature, not a guarantee about any individual bird or laboratory.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Ellegren, H. (1996). First gene on the avian W chromosome (CHD) provides a tag for universal sexing of non-ratite birds. <em>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</em>, 263(1377), 1635–1641. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.1996.0239\">doi:10.1098/rspb.1996.0239</a></li>\n<li>Griffiths, R., Double, M. C., Orr, K., &amp; Dawson, R. J. G. (1998). A DNA test to sex most birds. <em>Molecular Ecology</em>, 7(8), 1071–1075. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-294x.1998.00389.x\">doi:10.1046/j.1365-294x.1998.00389.x</a></li>\n<li>Fridolfsson, A.-K., &amp; Ellegren, H. (1999). A simple and universal method for molecular sexing of non-ratite birds. <em>Journal of Avian Biology</em>, 30(1), 116–121. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2307/3677252\">doi:10.2307/3677252</a></li>\n<li>Smith, C. A., Roeszler, K. N., Ohnesorg, T., et al. (2009). The avian Z-linked gene DMRT1 is required for male sex determination in the chicken. <em>Nature</em>, 461(7261), 267–271. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature08298\">doi:10.1038/nature08298</a></li>\n<li>Casey, A. E., Jones, K. L., Sandercock, B. K., &amp; Wisely, S. M. (2009). Heteroduplex molecules cause sexing errors in a standard molecular protocol for avian sexing. <em>Molecular Ecology Resources</em>, 9(1), 61–65. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-0998.2008.02307.x\">doi:10.1111/j.1755-0998.2008.02307.x</a></li>\n<li>Kroczak, A., Wołoszyńska, M., Wierzbicki, H., et al. (2021). New bird sexing strategy developed in the order Psittaciformes involves multiple markers to avoid sex misidentification: debunked myth of the universal DNA marker. <em>Genes</em>, 12(6), 878. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/genes12060878\">doi:10.3390/genes12060878</a></li>\n<li>Marc, S., Boldura, O. M., Paul, C., et al. (2025). Cross-species validation of pigeon-specific CHD1 primers for molecular sexing in pet birds. <em>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</em>, 26(22), 11142. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms262211142\">doi:10.3390/ijms262211142</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p>For parrot-specific sampling and turnaround, see the <a href=\"/dna-testing-for-parrot-gender/\">parrot gender testing page</a>; for the service menu, the <a href=\"/avian-gender-dna-test/\">bird DNA gender testing laboratory</a> lists accepted sample types, and the <a href=\"/articles/\">articles index</a> collects the rest of the series.</p>";
